  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container prose reveal">
      <h2>Why “Peace Antz”?</h2>
      <p>
        Watch an ant colony for a while and you’ll see something quietly
        miraculous: thousands of small creatures, none of them powerful alone,
        accomplishing things far beyond any individual — through cooperation,
        humility, and relentless effort. That picture has been the heart of
        Peace Antz from the beginning. Small actions, faithfully repeated,
        together become significant. <em>Be Kind. Be Relentless.</em>
      </p>

      <h2>Where we came from</h2>
      <p>
        Peace Antz began as a <strong>band</strong>. Before there was a
        website, a workshop, or a single line of code, there was music —
        friends playing together, most memorably at the beach sessions we
        called <em>Beach Jamz</em>. The name held the whole idea already:
        peace, and the humble, hardworking ant.
      </p>
      <p>
        From that musical root grew an era of experiments: a decentralized
        autonomous organization (DAO) with its own constitution, token, and
        ant-colony role system — alongside a workshop of practical ventures.
        We ran blockchain validators (including a community-recovery validator
        on Harmony), operated an on-demand 3D printing service that repaired
        household things and made lithophane art, and flew drones. Some of it
        worked, some of it didn’t, and all of it taught us. Those chapters are
        told honestly in <a href="/about/past-work">Past Work</a>, and the
        original DAO constitution is preserved in the
        <a href="/archive/constitution-v1">archive</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where we are now</h2>
      <p>
        The deepest thread running through all those experiments was never the
        technology — it was the longing for a more peaceful, connected,
        generous world. Today Peace Antz puts that first, explicitly:
        <strong>God first, service first, people first.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        We draw our inspiration from the Bahá’í teachings — unity of humanity,
        harmony of science and religion, work done in the spirit of service,
        deeds over words. You don’t need to be a Bahá’í to belong here; the
        <a href="/principles">principles</a> speak for themselves, and the door
        is open to people of every faith and background. We try to let conduct
        do the preaching.
      </p>

      <h2>What we make</h2>
      <p>
        Our first offering in this new chapter is small and real: the
        <a href="/create">Quote Card Creator</a>, a free tool for turning
        passages from the sacred writings into beautiful cards you can print,
        post, or give away. It grew out of our BahAI Workforce experiment — a
        team of AI agents that designs printed giveaway cards under strict,
        code-enforced honesty rules: every quote verified against the published
        writings, every piece of AI artwork labeled, no invented claims. We
        believe technology can serve the sacred, carefully.
      </p>

      <h2>Who runs this</h2>
      <p>
        Plainly: Peace Antz is stewarded by one person right now, with friends
        who help along the way. It is not (yet) a bustling organization, and
        this site doesn’t pretend otherwise. What it is, is a direction — and
        an invitation. The colony metaphor only works when there’s more than
        one ant. If something here resonates,
        <a href="/community">here’s how to take part</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
